# Security

## Reporting a vulnerability

Email: **{{ author_email }}** (or open a private security advisory on the repo). Please include:

- Affected version / commit
- Steps to reproduce
- Impact assessment (data exposure / privilege escalation / DoS / …)

We aim to acknowledge within 48h and ship a fix within 7 days for high-severity issues.

---

## Security model

### Authentication

{%- if use_jwt %}
- **JWT (`HS256`)** signed with `SECRET_KEY`. Access token TTL = `ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES` (default 30 min). Refresh token TTL = `REFRESH_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES` (default 7 days).
- **Password hashing:** Argon2id via `pwdlib` (as in the official FastAPI template). Hashes of earlier versions (bcrypt) are verified and replaced with Argon2id at the next sign-in. Plain passwords are never stored. A sign-in with an unknown address does the same hashing work, so response times do not reveal which addresses exist.
- **Secrets at rest:** connector credentials and MCP tokens are encrypted (Fernet) with `DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEYS`, separate from `SECRET_KEY` and rotatable (a new key goes in front; older ones still decrypt).
- **Logs:** messages, `extra=` fields and exception text are redacted (passwords, tokens, API key secrets, e-mail addresses); fields named like secrets are never written; SQL errors do not carry statement values outside debug mode. A test (`tests/test_log_trap.py`) fails when a known secret reaches the logs.
{%- if enable_oauth_google %}
- **OAuth 2.0 (Google)** — auth-code flow. Token validated server-side, internal user record looked up/created by email.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_session_management %}
- **Session management** — DB-backed sessions with revocation. Each refresh-token issuance creates a session row; `/sessions` endpoint lets users see + revoke devices.
{%- else %}
- **Stateless JWT** — no DB session table. Logout is client-side (drop tokens). For server-side revocation, regenerate with `--session-management`.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if use_api_key %}
- **Admin API key** — static `settings.API_KEY` matched via `X-API-Key` header for service-to-service calls. Constant-time compared with `secrets.compare_digest()`.
{%- endif %}

- **Single sign-on** — any OpenID Connect provider, configured at run time (`OIDC_PROVIDERS`): code flow with PKCE, ID token verified, an existing account is matched only by an address the provider has verified (or a trusted company directory). The frontend receives a one-time code, never tokens in a URL.
- **CI workload identity** — GitHub Actions and GitLab CI jobs exchange their OpenID Connect token, checked against `WORKLOAD_IDENTITIES` (issuer, audience, repository and branch claims), for a short-lived credential acting for one organization with API key scopes. No secret is stored in the CI.

### Authorization

- **Role-based** via `RoleChecker` dep (`UserRole.USER` / `UserRole.ADMIN`).
{%- if enable_admin_panel %}
- **Admin pages** require `role=admin`. Sensitive ops (impersonate user, system-health) gated separately.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_teams %}
- **Workspace scope** — every authenticated request resolves an `ActiveOrg` (default = personal org), after a membership check.
- **Org roles:** `OWNER` / `ADMIN` / `MEMBER` / `VIEWER`. Owner can transfer ownership + delete org.
- **Database segregation** — PostgreSQL row level security on every table, enforced for a separate application login that cannot bypass it. Each transaction states who acts (user, organization or API key); without a principal nothing is visible. Members' conversations stay private to them; roles are checked in the database too. Privileged work uses logged system sessions. See the documentation, Concepts > Tenancy and API keys.
{%- endif %}

### Transport / network

- **CORS** — origin list from `settings.CORS_ORIGINS`. Restrict to your domains in production.
- **HTTPS** — enforce via reverse proxy (Nginx / Traefik / ALB). Strict-Transport-Security header set in middleware when `ENVIRONMENT=production`.
- **CSP** — the API sets a restrictive Content Security Policy and `frame-ancestors 'none'`.{% if use_frontend %} The frontend's nginx sets its own policy (scripts from the same origin, API calls to `VITE_API_URL` only): `frontend/security-headers.conf`.{% endif %}

### Data

- **Secrets** — read from environment via `pydantic-settings`. Never committed. See `.env.example` + `ENV_VARS.md`.
{%- if enable_admin_features_audit_log %}
- **Audit log** — admin-mutating actions (user updates, deletes, impersonations, role changes) recorded in `app_admin_audit_log` table with actor + IP + payload snapshot.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_billing %}
- **Stripe webhooks** — signature verified via `stripe.Webhook.construct_event(secret=STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET)`. Idempotency table prevents replay.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_rag %}
- **RAG documents** — file uploads scoped per-org. No public read endpoint; all retrieval happens server-side during chat.
{%- endif %}

### Supply chain

- **Dependencies** — `pip-audit` (backend){% if use_frontend %} and `bun audit` (frontend){% endif %} fail the pipeline on known vulnerabilities; the container image is scanned with Trivy (fixable critical and high findings fail the build).
- **Code** — Ruff's security rules (`S`, from Bandit) run with the linter; reviewed exceptions are listed in `backend/pyproject.toml`.
- **Secrets in the repository** — gitleaks scans the whole history in the pipeline.
- **Agent skills** — the skills in `skills.lock.toml` are text an agent follows. Git sources are pinned to a commit and to the SHA-256 of their content; every skill is scanned before it is installed (hidden Unicode, instructions to override the agent, piping downloads into a shell, reading credential files block the install; scripts, binaries and unpinned package runs are reported). `python scripts/agent_skills.py scan` shows the findings.
- **Bill of materials** — a CycloneDX SBOM is built for every build of the default branch.

### Hardening checklist for production

- [ ] Rotate `SECRET_KEY` and `API_KEY` from generated defaults.
- [ ] Set `DEBUG=false` and `ENVIRONMENT=production`.
- [ ] Restrict `CORS_ORIGINS` to your domain(s).
{%- if enable_rate_limiting %}
- [ ] Tune `RATE_LIMIT_REQUESTS` / `RATE_LIMIT_PERIOD` in `.env`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_prometheus %}
- [ ] Set `PROMETHEUS_AUTH_TOKEN` if `/metrics` is exposed on a public endpoint.
{%- endif %}
{%- if enable_sentry %}
- [ ] Set `SENTRY_DSN` to ship errors. Verify PII scrubbing rules in `core/sentry.py`.
{%- endif %}
- [ ] Enforce HTTPS at the proxy layer.
- [ ] Run `pip-audit` / `bun audit` in CI for dependency vulnerabilities.
- [ ] Configure database backups + restore test schedule.
{%- if enable_billing %}
- [ ] Subscribe Stripe webhook to all relevant events; verify endpoint via Stripe CLI.
{%- endif %}

## Known limitations

- **No 2FA / MFA** for password accounts. Sign-in through an identity provider can enforce it there.
- **No SAML.** Single sign-on uses OpenID Connect (Google, Microsoft Entra ID, Keycloak, any issuer); an identity provider that speaks only SAML needs a broker such as Keycloak.
- **Log redaction is pattern-based** — passwords, tokens, keys and e-mail addresses are recognised by their name or format; a secret without either (a bare value in free text) is not. Keep secrets out of log calls.
{%- if use_jwt and not enable_session_management %}
- **No server-side session revocation** — JWTs valid until expiry. Compromised tokens require `SECRET_KEY` rotation (invalidates ALL sessions). Enable `--session-management` for selective revocation.
{%- endif %}
